Subject: [PATCH] perf(workers): defer Codex quota recovery during managed boot
 [daveey]

Problem: round4 natural post-load traces still spent 2.46s/1.91s in quota
recovery HTTPS; frozen original-query n55 median 7.435295s misses <=4s.
Why: the negative-only memo suppresses warm probes but every cold, stale,
invalid or changed-evidence miss falls through to synchronous HTTP. Fresh
workers cannot rely on that memo. Earlier coverage proved memo reuse, not
absence of network on construction, so the implementation contract was
incomplete despite local savings.

Scope deferral to authenticated managed Kanban construction, not environment
heuristics. Both quota GET and expired-token pre-probe refresh return
fail-closed without network in that scope. Queue one context-bound daemon
refresh against a separately loaded pool on scope exit. Reuse ordinary
refresh, cooldown proof and generation-aware persistence; never share
positive memo evidence or mutate the constructing agent's pool. Skip the
exception recovery retry only after a Codex recovery was actually deferred.
Other surfaces and ordinary recovery retain inline behavior. A daemon may
end with its worker; failure/unknown evidence leaves cooldowns intact and
ordinary external recovery remains available. No RPC or receipt fences move.

Proof: corrected fail-without-fix run 16 failed/5 passed, including all eight
cold/missing/stale/invalid x exhausted/healthy-peer controls, expired token
and constructor error retry. GREEN21; current consumer sweep70 files848
passed/5 skipped with no per-file retries; full21 static checks pass.
Controls cover newer failure during HTTP, 429/500/missing/closed windows,
ordinary inline recovery, eventual persisted recovery, usable-only boot,
nested exceptions, failed thread start and bounded real-thread deduplication.

No performance acceptance claimed: after exact-head review/CI/canonical
landing, await natural five-host loading and >=50 real original-query
sessions through one scheduler wake check. Preserve previous failed cohort,
per-route reporting, <=4s target and accepted no-RPC-change disposition.

